<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santiago de Liniers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1753–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Jacques de Liniers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Liniers was a French-born officer in Spanish service who led the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> against the British invasions of 1806 and 1807. His success with local militias made him a popular hero, and he became viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> from 1807 to 1809. Loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, he opposed the May Revolution of 1810 and was executed by the revolutionaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santiago-de-liniers.jpg" alt="Santiago de Liniers" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santiago_de_Liniers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liniers was born Jacques de Liniers at Niort in Poitou on 25 July 1753, the fourth son of Jacques Joseph Louis, comte de Liniers, a captain in the French Royal Navy. As a younger son unlikely to inherit, he entered the military academy of the Order of St. John at twelve and was commissioned in the Royal-Piémont cavalry regiment. In 1774 he left French service to volunteer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, which the third Pacte de Famille allowed, and became an ensign in the Spanish Navy in 1775.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-cevallos">Pedro de Cevallos</a> in 1776, fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, capturing two British ships laden with arms at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Port Mahon</a> in 1782, and negotiated the release of European prisoners with the king of Tripoli. In 1788 he was given command of a flotilla of gunboats in the Río de la Plata, and he married María Martina Sarratea, daughter of a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> merchant.</p><p>When a British force of 1,700 men took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in June 1806, Liniers raised 1,200 volunteers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> with its governor Pascual Ruiz Huidobro, landed on 4 August and retook the city after street fighting, receiving the surrender of General William Carr Beresford. An open cabildo made him commander-in-chief in place of Viceroy Rafael de Sobremonte, and he drafted every man able to bear arms, slaves included.</p><p>The next year he defeated John Whitelocke's attack with 11,000 soldiers. Officially appointed viceroy in May 1808 and made Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, he faced suspicion of his French birth once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, survived a mutiny led by Álzaga, and handed power without resistance to Baltasar Hidalgo de Cisneros, named by the Junta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Retired at Alta Gracia, Liniers joined the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in opposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primera-junta">Primera Junta</a> after the May Revolution. His army melted away before Ortiz de Ocampo's troops, and he was captured. When Ocampo refused to execute the prisoners, the Junta sent Juan José Castelli, and Liniers was executed without trial at Cabeza de Tigre on 26 August 1810. In 1861 Queen Isabella II had his remains buried in the Panteón de Marinos Ilustres at San Fernando, Cádiz. His house at Alta Gracia is a museum, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> neighbourhood bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Santiago de Liniers, 1st Count of Buenos Aires” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Córdoba's counter-revolution under the former viceroy <b>Santiago de Liniers</b> was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and Montevideo swore to the Regency and the Viceroyalty of Peru annexed Upper Peru, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1806 and 1807 British forces invaded Buenos Aires and Montevideo, and local militias under <b>Santiago de Liniers</b> drove them out, boosting Creole confidence.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Niort, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.33,-0.46&amp;year=1790" aria-label="Niort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4539 534 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Santiago de Liniers"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4989 804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4989 804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.33,-0.46&amp;year=1790">What was here in 1790? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
